# The admin panel's credentials. Copy this file to .env.local and fill it in.
#
# .env* is already in .gitignore, so .env.local stays out of the repository —
# which is the whole point: the DEFAULTS these replace are checked in, visible
# in lib/admin/config.ts, and refused outright by a production build.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SESSION_SECRET  (required in production)
#
# Signs the session cookie. Any change to it invalidates every signed-in
# session at once, which is also the only way to force a sign-out — there is no
# server-side session table. At least 32 characters; 64 is better and costs
# nothing.
#
#   openssl rand -base64 48
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(48).toString('base64'))"
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
SESSION_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ADMIN_EMAIL  (required in production)
#
# The one address that can sign in. Compared case-insensitively.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@hotcups.co.in
ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH=
ADMIN_PASSWORD=Admin@123


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE PASSWORD — set exactly ONE of the two below.
#
# ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH is the one to deploy. Generate it with:
#
#   npm run admin:hash "the password you want"
#
# and paste the whole pbkdf2$… string. The plaintext then exists nowhere on the
# server, so a leaked env dump does not hand anyone the password itself.
#
# !! ESCAPE THE DOLLARS IN THIS FILE. Next loads .env through dotenv-expand,
# !! which reads $NAME as a reference to another variable — and a PBKDF2 string
# !! is four fields separated by $. Pasted raw, "pbkdf2$210000$salt$hash" is
# !! loaded as "pbkdf2-salt-hash" and every sign-in fails with "that email and
# !! password do not match", which points at the password and says nothing
# !! about the file. Write it as pbkdf2\$210000\$salt\$hash here.
# !!
# !! A HOSTING DASHBOARD IS THE OPPOSITE: Vercel, Railway and the rest store
# !! what you type, so paste the UNESCAPED string there. `npm run admin:hash`
# !! prints both forms, each labelled.
#
# ADMIN_PASSWORD is the pragmatic alternative: the plaintext, which most hosts'
# environment-variable UIs make the easiest thing to paste. It is no weaker in
# transit — only in what a leaked dump reveals — and it is a perfectly
# reasonable choice for a site with one operator. Set one or the other, not
# both; the hash wins if both are present.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
